## Optimization Strategy (Phase 3F)

### Tick ingestion (50K rows/s peak)

**Rule 1 — Batch inserts, never single-row.** Adapter batches ticks into 100ms windows. `COPY` protocol via `pg` driver.

**Rule 2 — `synchronous_commit = off` for tick writer connection only.** Ticks are reconstructible from WebSocket on reconnect. Reduces fsync latency from ~5ms to ~0.1ms. All other connections keep `synchronous_commit = on`.

**Rule 3 — FK declared `NOT VALID`, validated nightly.** Constraint validation at 50K rows/s with FK lookup costs ~30% throughput. Adapter guarantees `symbol_id` validity before insert.

### Position MTM updates

Use `UPDATE ... WHERE symbol_id = $1 AND status IN ('open','partial')` with `idx_positions_active` partial index. Update only three computed columns — never full row. Portfolio aggregate recomputed every 5 seconds via scheduled service call, not on every tick.

### Candle queries (backtesting, chart rendering)

Always include `timeframe` in WHERE clause — without it, the planner scans all timeframe partitions for the time range. Use covering index `idx_candles_backtest` — no heap fetch.

For backtests needing 2+ years of daily candles: use `equity_curve_1d` continuous aggregate, not raw `candles`. Pre-aggregated; 100× fewer rows.

### Signal engine reads (sub-10ms requirement)

Read `strategy_performance` and `regime_performance` with `ORDER BY period_end DESC LIMIT 1`. Parse `indicator_rankings` JSONB in TypeScript application layer — never use `jsonb_array_elements()` on hot path.

### PgBouncer configuration

Transaction-mode pooling. Pool size = `(2 × CPU cores) + 1`. Tick writer: dedicated pool of 3 connections. All other app connections: shared pool of 10. Read replica: separate pool of 5 for backtest/analytical queries.

