## Security Considerations (Phase 3G)

### SEC1 — Token encryption (CRITICAL)

`broker_sessions` stores `access_token`, `refresh_token`, `feed_token` — bearer credentials that allow full account impersonation. Encrypt at application layer using `pgp_sym_encrypt` before insert; decrypt on read. Encryption key in environment variables, never in DB. Applies to all three token fields.

### SEC2 — Row-level security

Enable PostgreSQL RLS on `trades`, `positions`, `orders`, `executions`, `portfolios`. App role sees only rows matching its `account_id`. DB enforces it — not just the application. Prevents data exposure from query bugs that drop the `WHERE account_id = $1` filter.

### SEC3 — Role separation

Three database roles required:

| Role | Privileges | Used by |
|---|---|---|
| `artha_writer` | INSERT/UPDATE on trades/orders/executions/positions; INSERT on ticks/candles | Running application |
| `artha_reader` | SELECT only | Read replica, backtest queries, report generation |
| `artha_admin` | Schema migrations only | Migration runner; never active application |

REVOKE all excess privileges. `artha_reader` must not have SELECT on `broker_token`, `broker_exch_type` (SEC5).

### SEC4 — Audit chain key management

`audit_log.chain_hash` uses `pgcrypto`. Key rotation requires `key_versions` table: `(key_id, activated_at, deprecated_at)`. Store `key_id` on each `audit_log` row. Old rows remain verifiable with original key; new rows use current key.

### SEC5 — `broker_token` access restriction

`symbols.broker_token` and `broker_exch_type` combined with a SmartAPI session allow subscription to any instrument. Apply column-level REVOKE on both columns for `artha_reader` role.

### SEC6 — Connection encryption

`pg_hba.conf` must require `scram-sha-256` authentication and `sslmode=require` for all remote connections. PgBouncer → PostgreSQL uses `peer` auth (localhost only). Deployment pre-flight check before Phase 4.

