## Scalability Considerations (Phase 3G)

### SC1 — Single TimescaleDB node write bottleneck

At 50K ticks/s with 1500+ subscribed symbols (full NSE F&O), single-node PostgreSQL will saturate at ~6–12 months of growth. **Mitigation:** keep subscriptions scoped to active watchlist symbols (< 200 at launch). Re-evaluate when sustained tick volume exceeds 10K/s.

### SC2 — `option_strikes` write volume underestimated

Full NIFTY + BANKNIFTY chain (400+ strikes) at 5-second capture intervals = 1.7M rows/trading day for two underlyings. `option_strikes` is not a TimescaleDB hypertable. **Mitigation:** add `pg_partman` range partition by `captured_at` month before Phase 6 (live options).

### SC3 — `performance_metrics` EOD job linear growth

At 500 strategy runs, EOD recompute = 10K+ upserts. **Mitigation:** `statement_timeout = '5min'` on the job; async queue for overflow.

### SC4 — `learning_records.features_at_entry` JSONB size

At 50 trades/day × 200 features × 3 years = 5.5M rows with large JSONB blobs. **Mitigation:** cap feature vector at 64 named fields; schema-validate at application layer before insert.

### SC5 — Read replica lag during backtest write storms

Walk-forward backtesting writes thousands of rows in tight loops. Read replica lag can reach seconds. **Mitigation:** backtest writes go to a `backtest` schema on an isolated connection pool — never the main writer. Enforced at connection-pool level in Phase 4.

